  "content": {
    "id": "p19-q6-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "6",
    "prompt": "Prove that there exists a single point $(u,v)$ belonging to every quadratic function of the form $y=2x^2+ax+3a$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$(-3,18)$.\n\nEquate two members with distinct coefficients to obtain $x=-3$, then substitute to obtain $y=18$, independently of $a$.",
      "worked": "Choose distinct coefficients $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$. Any point common to every member must satisfy\n$$\\begin{align*}2x^2+\\alpha x+3\\alpha&=2x^2+\\beta x+3\\beta\\\\(\\alpha-\\beta)x&=-3(\\alpha-\\beta)\\\\x&=-3.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $\\alpha\\ne\\beta$, this is the only possible common $x$-coordinate. For every value of $a$,\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=2(-3)^2+a(-3)+3a\\\\y&=18.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $(-3,18)$ belongs to every member of the family and is the unique common point.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 65
  },
